kbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213261" :question/problem "![](images/de/ded4e8a94730585809c5534fee8d401481c919d529a67cd088e96b8797f2b237.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{2,5,8\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(2,8)$\n\nIII. $(8,5)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $2$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,8)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $8.$\n\n- The pair $(8,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $8$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048710 :topic/id #uuid "b73e30a4-d62d-555b-a6f7-1a03dfa6cf83" :topic/math-academy-id 4837 :topic/title "Graphical Representations of Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76eb6889-3903-5edc-98cf-ffd76ae6f40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :d