5499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472927} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb268383-61d6-5ba6-b042-e4302740ef46" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" :question/math-academy-id "q-213262" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n\nII. $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n\nIII. $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair  $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\beta $ and ends at the node $\\gamma .$\n\n- The pair $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\gamma $ and ends at the node $\\alpha .$\n\n- The pair $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a an arrow that starts at the node $\\alpha $ and ends at the node $\\beta .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-