on.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" :question/math-academy-id "q-139814" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "352a7d2c-4aba-5246-8a20-ed79de007b95" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Matrix Expression Involving the Hadamard Product is Well-Defined" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048122 :topic/id #uuid "80c022c4-6a8a-5129-a159-ed3a1eacb632" :topic/math-academy-id 5916 :topic/title "The Hadamard Product"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1a4696d-b69f-5d96-8a99-a5ac54f14d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #e